   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.3",
    "topic_title": "Estimating Derivatives of a Function at a Point",
    "stem": "The temperature T(t), in degrees Celsius, of a liquid being heated is recorded at selected times t, in minutes. At t = 0, 2, 5, 6, and 9, the values of T(t) are 20, 26, 35, 38, and 44, respectively. T is differentiable for 0 ≤ t ≤ 9. Using the data, what is the best estimate of T′(5.5)?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "8/3 degrees Celsius per minute",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This uses the distant endpoints, (44 − 20)/(9 − 0) = 24/9 = 8/3, which is the average rate over the whole interval. Over nine minutes the rate changes, so it poorly describes the rate near t = 5.5. Fix: Pick the two table values that bracket the target time most tightly."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "1/3 minute per degree Celsius",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This inverts the difference quotient, dividing the change in time by the change in temperature. T′ measures change in T per unit change in t, so temperature change belongs in the numerator. Fix: Write Δ(output)/Δ(input) and check the units read degrees Celsius per minute."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "3 degrees Celsius per minute",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The data points closest to t = 5.5 on either side are t = 5 and t = 6, so the difference quotient (38 − 35)/(6 − 5) = 3 approximates the local slope there. The units are output units per input unit, degrees Celsius per minute."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "36.5 degrees Celsius",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This averages T(5) and T(6), which estimates the temperature T(5.5), not its rate of change. A derivative estimate needs a difference quotient, not an average of function values. Fix: Ask whether the question wants a value of T or of T′ before computing."
     }
    ]
   },
